# The oracle meant to outlive the others.
#
# As game.c converts to real C, the block-head trace goes first and the memory
# hashes go when a variable's storage leaves emulated RAM. What is left is the
# screen -- and the screen belongs to the machine no matter who computed it.
#
# Why not frame hashes. verify.sh hashes video state at every host frame
# boundary, which is a wall-clock instant, and the two engines' cycle
# accounting is a few cycles out of phase over a run (22,147,953 against
# 22,147,957 -- measured, decision log 2026-08-15). Eight of 1,300 frames
# therefore sample at slightly different points in the program and differ, with
# nothing wrong in the machine. That makes an exact interpreter-vs-generated
# frame comparison impossible.
#
# Sampling at a program-defined instant removes the phase entirely, the same
# way probe-stamped keys removed it from input. $6217 is the in-game keyboard
# ingest: reached thousands of times, only while playing, and both engines
# reach it at exactly the same points because that is what the block-head trace
# proves today and what this inherits.
#
# This is a strict subset of ram.probe, which already hashes these ranges among
# others. It exists separately so that when the rest of memory stops matching,
# the check that remains is already written, already committed, and already
# known to pass.
counter n

probe kb() {
  inc n
  key n
}

probe screen(
    txt = hash($0400, $07FF),
    hgr1 = hash($2000, $3FFF),
    hgr2 = hash($4000, $5FFF)) {
  printf("%08X %08X %08X\n", txt, hgr1, hgr2)
}

install kb at $FD1B, $741F, $7890, $6217, $760F, $FB7C, $FD26
install screen at $6217
